<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/055Z">Definition <span data-tag="055Z">3.5.9.1</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a morphism of Kan complexes and let $n \geq -1$ be an integer. We say that $f$ is <em>$n$-truncated</em> if, for every vertex $x \in X$ having image $y = f(x)$, the induced map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \pi _{m}(f): \pi _{m}(X,x) \rightarrow \pi _{m}(Y,y) \]
</div>
<p> is injective for $m = n+1$ and bijective for $m &gt; n+1$. If $n \leq -2$, we say that $f$ is <em>$n$-truncated</em> if it is $(-1)$-truncated and the map $\pi _0(f): \pi _0(X) \rightarrow \pi _0(Y)$ is surjective. </p>
</article>
